    "question": {
      ":question/problem": "Calculate the integral $\\int \\frac{{5}^{x}}{{5}^{x}+5}\\,\\text{d}x.$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that the numerator is proportional to the derivative of the denominator. So, we introduce the new variable $u={5}^{x}+5.$ Differentiating gives \n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}={5}^{x}\\operatorname{ln}⁡5\\,⟹\\,\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\,\\text{d}u={5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{{5}^{x}}{{5}^{x}+5}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{{5}^{x}+5}\\cdot {5}^{x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\int \\frac{1}{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|u|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}\\cdot \\operatorname{ln}⁡|{5}^{x}+5|+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x}+5)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡5}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that, in the last step, we used the fact that ${5}^{x}+5>0$ for all $x.$",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/hard",
      ":question/id": "3bf40b70-b1f6-5ac1-aaeb-ebd31e9df950",
